export const BUILTIN_GUIDE_CONTENT = "# Project Delivery Leadership — Senior Project Manager Operating Guide\n\n*Version 1.2. Licensed under the European Union Public Licence v1.2 (see License).*\n\n## About this guide\n\nThis is an original, directive operating guide for senior project managers. It synthesizes widely taught, general project-delivery practice and is anchored to public, openly citable sources — including ISO 21500/21502, ISO 31000, ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148, ANSI/EIA-748, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, ISO/IEC 42001, and the OECD AI Principles (see References). It is organized in its own way and written in its own words; it does not reproduce, paraphrase, or copy the structure of any single proprietary standard, and it uses no third-party trademarks as headings or branding. The earned-value and earned-schedule formulas in §6 are standard mathematical definitions common across the field.\n\n**How to use.** Treat the principles (§1) as always-on. Run the lifecycle (§2) and manage the core disciplines (§3) at a depth proportional to the work. Use the deep modules — requirements and business analysis (§5), earned value (§6), benefits (§7), AI (§11), and program/portfolio (§12) — when the work calls for them. Use the \"Done when\" lines and the Definition of Done (§13) as checks. Tailor everything (§8).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operating principles\n\nApply all eight on every engagement.\n\n1. **Own the outcome.** Be accountable for the result and the decisions taken to reach it — not just for activity or output. Escalate honestly; do not let problems age.\n2. **Lead with integrity and earn trust.** Be honest, fair, and consistent. Make commitments you can keep, and keep the ones you make. Create an environment where people can speak up safely.\n3. **Keep value and benefits in front.** Manage toward the change the work is meant to produce, not just toward deliverables. Re-check whether the work is still worth doing; recommend stopping it when it is not.\n4. **Make quality a built-in habit, not an inspection.** Define what \"good\" means up front, build it into how the work is done, and catch defects early rather than at the end.\n5. **Treat uncertainty as work, not surprise.** Identify what could go wrong or right, decide responses in advance, and revisit them as you learn.\n6. **Engage the people the work affects.** Find them early, understand what they need, communicate deliberately, and keep them aligned as things change.\n7. **Right-size the method to the situation.** Choose how much process, planning, and formality the work actually needs. More is not better; fit is better.\n8. **Deliver responsibly.** Weigh environmental, social, and economic impact; respect law, ethics, and the people doing the work.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The delivery lifecycle\n\nFive phases. They overlap and repeat; in iterative work you cycle through plan–execute–steer many times.\n\n- **Initiate.** Establish why the work exists: the problem or opportunity, the intended outcome, success criteria, sponsor, budget envelope, and constraints. Confirm there is a viable case before committing resources. Authorize the work and name the people accountable.\n- **Plan.** Decide the approach, then plan to the level the approach needs: what will be delivered, by when, at what cost, with what people, against which risks and quality targets. Agree how you will track progress and control change. Plan progressively where detail is not yet knowable.\n- **Execute.** Direct and coordinate the work to produce the agreed outcomes. Lead the team; remove blockers; keep suppliers and dependencies moving.\n- **Steer (monitor and adapt).** Measure against the plan, forecast where you are heading, and act on the gap. Control scope, schedule, cost, quality, and risk. Run disciplined change control. Re-confirm value.\n- **Close.** Confirm acceptance, hand over deliverables and ownership, settle contracts, release the team, and capture what was learned. Make sure someone owns the benefits that land after you leave.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core disciplines (quick reference)\n\nManage each at a depth proportional to the work. Each has a short set of directives and a \"Done when\" check; the areas that reward more depth have their own modules later.\n\n### 3.1 Outcomes and value\nDefine the intended benefits and how they will be measured (in money or in clear qualitative terms). Trace every piece of work to a benefit. Cut work that does not serve one. (Depth: §7.)\n*Done when:* benefits are defined, measured, and on a credible path; no work exists that serves none.\n\n### 3.2 Scope and requirements\nEstablish the real need before choosing a solution. Elicit, analyze, and record requirements; set clear acceptance criteria; maintain traceability from need to deliverable to test. Control scope to prevent creep and over-build. (Depth: §5.)\n*Done when:* the need is documented, requirements are agreed and traced, and only necessary work is in scope.\n\n### 3.3 Schedule\nBuild a realistic schedule of activities, dependencies, durations, and milestones; detail near-term work and outline the rest, refining as you go. Track progress, forecast completion, and protect committed dates with timely action.\n*Done when:* a realistic, maintained schedule exists and forecasts are managed against it.\n\n### 3.4 Cost and funding\nEstimate costs, set a time-phased budget and funding plan, and separate reserves for known risks (contingency) from reserves for the unforeseen (management). Track spend against plan, forecast the final cost, and act on variances. (Depth: §6.)\n*Done when:* spend and forecast are reconciled to the budget and the case remains viable.\n\n### 3.5 Quality\nState acceptance criteria and a definition of done; build prevention in (reviews, standards, testing); reduce rework and waste; improve the process as you learn. Apply quality to both the deliverables and the way you produce them.\n*Done when:* deliverables meet acceptance criteria and the producing process is sound.\n\n### 3.6 Risk and uncertainty\nSet risk appetite and thresholds. Identify threats and opportunities as cause → event → effect. Assess and prioritize them; plan responses (for threats: avoid, reduce, transfer, accept, escalate; for opportunities: pursue, increase, share, accept, escalate); execute the responses; monitor and re-plan. Distinguish risks (possible, future) from issues (real, now).\n*Done when:* significant threats are handled and opportunities pursued in proportion; overall exposure is within appetite.\n\n### 3.7 Stakeholders and communication\nIdentify everyone affected — supporters, neutrals, and opponents — and their interest and influence. Plan engagement and communication: who needs what, through which channel, how often. Manage expectations, build credibility, and adjust as influence shifts.\n*Done when:* stakeholders are engaged at the right time and expectations are aligned through close.\n\n### 3.8 People and resources\nPlan, secure, and develop the people and the physical/virtual resources the work needs. Lead the team: set direction, give feedback, resolve or escalate conflict, and grow capability. Track availability and use; resolve contention.\n*Done when:* the right resources are available when needed and the team is capable and motivated.\n\n### 3.9 Suppliers and procurement\nDecide make-versus-buy early. Choose contract types that fit the risk. Select suppliers on clear criteria; manage performance, claims, and relationships; ensure ethics and compliance in sourcing.\n*Done when:* external work is sourced on fit-for-purpose terms and managed to deliver.\n\n### 3.10 Change control\nRun one disciplined path for changes: capture, assess impact (on scope, schedule, cost, risk, quality, benefits), decide, and update the baseline. Reject silent scope drift.\n*Done when:* every accepted change is assessed, decided, and reflected in the baseline.\n\n### 3.11 Governance and decision rights\nSet the right-sized framework: who decides what, which gates apply, how performance is reported, and how the work stays aligned to strategy. Make governance enable delivery, not bureaucratize it. Build in periodic review and lessons.\n*Done when:* decisions are made at the right level and the work stays aligned and accountable.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Knowledge and lessons\n\nCapture decisions, assumptions, and lessons as you go, and reuse them. Make learning a routine, not a closeout afterthought — write down what worked and what did not while it is fresh, and feed it forward to the team and the next effort.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Requirements and business analysis\n\nMost delivery failures trace back to building the wrong thing, not building badly. Treat requirements work as a discipline in its own right. On larger efforts a dedicated business analyst performs it; you own the outcome and partner with them. This module reflects general requirements-engineering and business-analysis practice (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148; BABOK).\n\n### 5.1 Establish the need before the solution\nRequire a documented **needs assessment** before committing to a solution: the problem or opportunity, the current state versus the desired state, the options considered, and the recommended solution scope, justified by value. Reject solution-first framing.\n\n### 5.2 The four requirement types — define and maintain all of them\n- **Business requirements** — higher-level organizational needs, goals, and outcomes (the *why*).\n- **Stakeholder requirements** — what specific stakeholders or groups need (the *who*).\n- **Solution requirements** — **functional** (what the solution must do) and **non-functional** (how well — performance, security, usability, reliability, accessibility).\n- **Transition requirements** — temporary capabilities needed to move from the current to the future state (migration, training, cutover, parallel running).\n\n### 5.3 The requirements activities\nRun these iteratively, not once:\n- **Plan** the approach — how requirements will be elicited, analyzed, prioritized, validated, and managed; agree the split of work between you and the analyst.\n- **Elicit** — draw out needs using fit-for-purpose techniques (interviews, workshops, observation, document analysis, prototypes); move items from assumed to confirmed.\n- **Analyze and model** — structure, decompose, and model requirements (process flows, data, states, user stories); resolve conflicts and gaps; define acceptance criteria.\n- **Specify** — record requirements clearly and testably; a good requirement is necessary, unambiguous, consistent, complete, feasible, and verifiable.\n- **Prioritize** — order by value, risk, dependency, and cost; in adaptive work, keep a ranked backlog.\n- **Validate and verify** — confirm requirements reflect the real need (validation) and are correctly built (verification).\n- **Manage and trace** — baseline requirements; maintain **bidirectional traceability** from business need → requirement → design → deliverable → test; control change through it.\n\n### 5.4 Evaluate the solution\nEvaluate the solution **before and after release**: confirm it meets the business need and delivers expected value; measure performance against value targets; recommend continue, adjust, or retire. Feed results into benefits tracking (§7).\n\n### 5.5 Analyst capabilities\nA strong analyst combines analytical skill (modeling, decomposition, critical thinking), domain and product knowledge, communication and facilitation, and professional behavior. Where you fill this role yourself, hold the same bar.\n\n### 5.6 Role boundary with the project manager\nAgree at the start who owns requirements, traceability, change, and solution evaluation; document it to avoid gaps and overlaps. The analyst owns requirements quality and solution fit; you own delivery of the outcome. Collaborate on scope, change control, and stakeholder engagement.\n\n*Done when:* a documented need precedes the solution; requirements of all four types are agreed, traced, and validated; value is defined and measured; the solution is evaluated against it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Measuring performance with earned value\n\nEarned value management (EVM) is a widely used, non-proprietary technique that integrates scope, schedule, and cost into a single, forecastable picture. Use it where you can set a stable baseline; adapt it (§6.6) for iterative work. The relationships below are standard across the field (ANSI/EIA-748; earned-schedule literature).\n\n### 6.1 Conditions for it to work\nDo not rely on EVM unless: leadership supports it and grants authority; the team is trained and committed; scope, schedule, and cost are integrated into one baseline; objective progress-claiming rules are set *before* execution; and the measurement system is maintained as a standing organizational capability. Treat it as a system, not a spreadsheet.\n\n### 6.2 Build the measurement baseline\n1. **Scope** — decompose the work into a work breakdown structure (WBS) down to work packages.\n2. **Responsibility** — build an organizational breakdown (OBS) and intersect it with the WBS so every **control account** has one accountable owner (a responsibility assignment matrix).\n3. **Schedule** — sequence activities, set durations, dependencies, and milestones; detail near-term work and hold far-term work in planning packages (rolling-wave).\n4. **Cost** — time-phase the budget to produce planned value by period; the total budgeted cost is the Budget at Completion (BAC).\n5. **Reserves** — keep **management reserve** outside the baseline for the unforeseen; hold **contingency** for identified risks at control-account or package level.\n\n### 6.3 The three measured values (to a chosen date)\n- **PV** — Planned Value: budgeted cost of the work *scheduled*.\n- **EV** — Earned Value: budgeted cost of the work *done*.\n- **AC** — Actual Cost: actual cost of the work *done*.\n- **BAC** — Budget at Completion: total budgeted cost of the work.\n\n### 6.4 Variances, indices, and forecasts (ASCII operators, safe to copy)\n\n```\nSchedule Variance         SV   = EV - PV         ( + ahead | 0 on | - behind schedule )\nCost Variance             CV   = EV - AC         ( + under | 0 on | - over cost )\nSchedule Perf. Index      SPI  = EV / PV         ( >1 ahead | =1 on | <1 behind )\nCost Perf. Index          CPI  = EV / AC         ( >1 under | =1 on | <1 over cost )\n\nEstimate at Completion    EAC  = BAC / CPI                    ( current cost efficiency continues )\n                          EAC  = AC + (BAC - EV)              ( remainder runs at planned rate )\n                          EAC  = AC + (BAC - EV)/(CPI * SPI)  ( cost AND schedule drive the rest )\n                          EAC  = AC + new bottom-up ETC       ( old estimate no longer valid )\nEstimate to Complete      ETC  = EAC - AC\nVariance at Completion    VAC  = BAC - EAC        ( + under | - over final cost )\nTo-Complete Perf. Index   TCPI = (BAC - EV)/(BAC - AC)   ( efficiency needed to finish on budget )\n                          TCPI = (BAC - EV)/(EAC - AC)   ( efficiency needed to finish at the EAC )\n                          ( >1 harder | =1 same | <1 easier than performance so far )\n```\n\n**Read it, don't just report it.** CPI below 1 means you get less than a unit of work per unit of cost. If the efficiency needed to finish on budget (TCPI) is far above the efficiency you have shown (CPI), the plan is probably no longer achievable — revise the forecast and escalate.\n\n### 6.5 Earned schedule (schedule performance in time)\nCost-based schedule metrics fail late in a project: at completion SV is always 0 and SPI always 1, even if you finished late. **Earned schedule (ES)** fixes this by expressing schedule performance in *time*. ES is the point in time at which the EV you have earned *should* have been earned per the baseline — found where the PV curve equals current EV.\n\n```\nEarned Schedule           ES   = C + (EV - PV_C) / (PV_C+1 - PV_C)\n                          ( C = last period where cumulative PV <= EV; interpolate into the next )\nTime Schedule Variance    SV(t)  = ES - AT          ( AT = actual time elapsed )\nTime Schedule Index       SPI(t) = ES / AT\nTime estimate at compl.   IEAC(t) = PD / SPI(t)     ( PD = planned duration )\n```\n\nUnlike SPI, SPI(t) does not drift to 1 as work finishes, so it stays meaningful for forecasting completion dates. ES needs no new data — only these formulas.\n\n### 6.6 Iterative / hybrid work\nDo not force a fixed-scope baseline onto exploratory work. Map iterative work into one or more control accounts (e.g., features as work packages, releases or sprints as the measurement point), measure cost and schedule efficiency at that level, and convert the data into common units for consolidated reporting. Late in delivery, prefer the time-based metrics in §6.5.\n\n### 6.7 Pair EVM with risk\nEVM extrapolates the past; risk management scans the future. Use each to sharpen the other: let risk exposure inform reserves and forecasts, and let performance trends trigger risk review. Fund forecast adjustments from analysis, not optimism.\n\n*Done when:* the baseline is integrated and authorized; variances are explained with corrective actions; EAC/VAC are reconciled to the business case; TCPI is assessed against the funding goal.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Benefits realization\n\nProjects produce **outputs and capabilities**; value comes from the **benefits** those enable — often after delivery. Manage benefits as a lifecycle, not a closing slide. This module reflects general benefits-management practice (ISO 21502; Managing Successful Programmes; Cranfield benefits-management work).\n\n### 7.1 The benefits lifecycle\n1. **Identify.** Name the intended benefits and dis-benefits; assign each a beneficiary; confirm each supports a strategic objective. Record them in a **benefits register** and, where useful, a **benefits map** linking outputs → capabilities → outcomes → benefits → objectives.\n2. **Define and quantify.** Make each benefit measurable: a metric, a baseline, a target, and a timeframe. Distinguish tangible (financial, time) from intangible (satisfaction, reputation) and state how each will be evidenced.\n3. **Plan.** Sequence when benefits land relative to deliverables and operational changes; name an owner for each; identify what must change in the business for the benefit to materialize.\n4. **Realize.** Track leading indicators during and after delivery; remove barriers; note that benefits often accrue incrementally and may continue past project close.\n5. **Transition and embed.** Hand over deliverables, capabilities, and benefit-tracking ownership to operations or the beneficiary; prepare them for the change so the benefit can actually be taken.\n6. **Sustain and review.** Confirm benefits persist after handover; measure actuals against targets; feed lessons back into future investment decisions.\n\n### 7.2 Your responsibility as the project manager\nDo not claim benefits your project cannot itself realize. Deliver the outputs and capabilities, make the benefit owners and measures explicit, and ensure tracking continues after you leave. Where you sit inside a program, the program usually owns end-to-end realization (§12).\n\n*Done when:* benefits are defined and measured with named owners, the realization path survives project close, and a review of actuals is scheduled.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Choosing and tailoring the approach\n\nPick the delivery approach deliberately, then tailor process to fit.\n\n- **Predictive** when requirements are stable and well understood and the cost of change is high. Plan thoroughly up front; control to baselines.\n- **Iterative / agile** when requirements are uncertain or expected to evolve and feedback is valuable. Deliver in increments; let scope flex within time and cost; replan often.\n- **Hybrid** when different parts of the work suit different approaches. Combine them deliberately at the boundary.\n- **Set a delivery cadence** (one delivery, periodic deliveries, or continuous) that matches when value is realized.\n\n**Tailor in three moves:** start from the approach the work suggests; adjust for the organization (its constraints, policies, and maturity); adjust for the specific project (size, complexity, criticality, risk, stakeholders). Then keep adjusting — inspect and improve the approach as you go. More process is not the goal; the least that reliably works is.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Leading the team and yourself\n\n### 9.1 Leadership behaviors\n- Set clear direction and decision rights; agree team working norms at the start.\n- Adapt your style to the situation and the people without abandoning your principles.\n- Share leadership — let the right person lead in the moment; grow other leaders.\n- Give direct, timely feedback; resolve conflict early; remove obstacles.\n- Build a team that reflects diverse perspectives and uses them well.\n\n### 9.2 Capabilities to build (in yourself and the team)\nA capable senior PM develops five areas, deliberately and continuously:\n- **Ways of working** — methods, planning, and delivery craft across predictive, agile, and hybrid.\n- **Business and strategic acumen** — connecting the work to strategy, finance, and the organization's context.\n- **Interpersonal and leadership skill** — communication, negotiation, influence, emotional intelligence, conflict resolution.\n- **Delivering results** — problem-solving, decision-making under uncertainty, and getting outcomes in real organizations.\n- **Ethics and responsibility** — integrity, fairness, and the social and environmental dimension (§10).\n\nDevelop capability in the flow of work: mix experience levels for mentoring, identify learning needs, coach, and create stretch with support.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Delivering sustainably and responsibly\n\n- Consider environmental, social, and economic impact across the life of the work, and prefer, in order: **avoid** harm, then **reduce** it, then **restore** or **offset** what remains.\n- Put sustainability and ethics into the case, the requirements, and the contracts — and raise them even when they are missing from formal documents.\n- Hold yourself, the team, and the sponsor jointly responsible for the impact of what you build.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Responsible use of AI in delivery\n\nWhen you use AI to help run the work, or deliver AI as part of the product, add the controls below. They align with public AI-governance frameworks: the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (which organizes work into the functions **govern, map, measure, manage**), ISO/IEC 42001 (an AI management-system standard), and the OECD AI Principles; risk-tiered regulation such as the EU AI Act may also apply.\n\n### 11.1 Distinguish AI as a tool from AI as a deliverable\nGovern both, with stricter controls on AI you deliver. For AI-as-tool (e.g., drafting, analysis, forecasting), focus on output quality and human review. For AI-as-deliverable, add full lifecycle, architecture, and assurance controls.\n\n### 11.2 The AI lifecycle to govern\nScope and frame → source and prepare data → build and validate the model → deploy → monitor → retire. Apply the controls below across all stages, and re-validate after material change.\n\n### 11.3 Controls\n- **Keep a human in the loop.** Do not let automation make consequential decisions alone. Require human review of AI output in ambiguous, complex, or high-stakes situations; AI cannot weigh ethics, context, or interpersonal nuance, and can be confidently wrong or biased. Define which decisions need human sign-off.\n- **Govern.** Set policy, roles, accountability, and oversight for AI use; decide where AI is and is not appropriate; use it for value, not novelty.\n- **Manage data quality.** Trustworthy output depends on accurate, representative, current, and secure data. Check provenance; manage bias; protect privacy.\n- **Design for quality and reliability.** Set quality targets for AI components (accuracy, robustness, reliability) and validate against them before release.\n- **Be transparent and explainable.** Disclose where and how AI is used; make decisions explainable to the degree the context demands.\n- **Assign accountability.** Name an owner for each AI use across its lifecycle; AI does not absorb responsibility.\n- **Manage fairness and bias.** Test for and mitigate discriminatory or skewed outcomes.\n- **Protect privacy and security.** Guard against data leakage, model misuse, and adversarial attack.\n- **Monitor and manage drift.** Watch performance over time; detect model and data drift; respond and re-validate.\n- **Lead the people and culture change.** Build readiness, trust, and capability; manage the change AI introduces to roles and ways of working.\n- **Meet compliance.** Map applicable law, regulation, and policy; document conformance.\n\n*Done when:* human-in-the-loop controls operate for consequential decisions; data quality, fairness, privacy, and security are addressed; AI use is transparent, accountable, monitored, and compliant.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Operating within programs and portfolios\n\nA senior PM usually operates inside larger structures. Understand both layers and manage the interfaces (general practice; ISO 21502 covers project, programme, and portfolio management).\n\n### 12.1 The program layer (related work, collective benefit)\nA **program** coordinates related projects and change activity to deliver benefits and outcomes that the projects could not deliver alone. Programs carry more uncertainty, change, and complexity than projects, and are organized around **benefits**, not deliverables. Program-level disciplines to expect:\n- **Strategic alignment** — keep every component traceable to the strategic objective; maintain the program case and roadmap.\n- **Benefits management** — run the benefits lifecycle (§7) end to end, across components and into sustainment.\n- **Stakeholder engagement** — over a larger, longer-horizon, more diverse stakeholder set.\n- **Governance** — authorize components, run stage reviews, oversee benefits and risk.\n- **Dependency and synergy management** — resolve cross-component dependencies; make the whole worth more than the sum.\n- **Aggregate risk, change, finance, and resources** — managed across the program.\n\nA program typically runs **define → deliver → close**, delivering and transitioning benefits iteratively through the delivery phase.\n\n### 12.2 The portfolio layer (the right work, at the right time)\nA **portfolio** is a collection of programs, projects, and other work managed together to achieve strategic objectives and to balance value against risk and capacity. Components need not be related; they compete for the same funding and resources. Portfolio decision areas:\n- **Strategic alignment** — translate strategy into selection criteria and a portfolio roadmap.\n- **Governance** — authorize, oversee, and decide continue/hold/stop.\n- **Value management** — define, measure, and maximize portfolio value.\n- **Risk and risk appetite** — balance aggregate value against aggregate risk within a stated appetite.\n- **Capacity and capability** — match demand to the supply of resources and skills; sequence activation to capacity.\n- **Stakeholder engagement** — with senior leaders, sponsors, and component managers.\n\nA portfolio is managed continuously — **define → plan → authorize and execute → optimize → monitor and control** — with ongoing **intake, evaluation, selection, prioritization, and balancing** of components against value, risk, and capacity.\n\n### 12.3 Your interface as a project manager\n- **Stay aligned.** Take your mandate, case, and success criteria from the wider strategy; keep your work traceable to it; flag drift.\n- **Hand off benefits.** Transfer deliverables, ownership, and benefit tracking at close; do not claim benefits realized above your level.\n- **Surface dependencies.** Make interdependencies and contention visible to the program; support shared outcomes.\n- **Report upward honestly.** Give those who select and balance investments what they need — value, cost, risk, resource demand, and performance (including EVM, §6) — and accept that your work may be re-sequenced, re-funded, or stopped on strategic grounds.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Definition of done — engagement checklist\n\n- [ ] Intended outcomes and value achieved or exceeded; benefit ownership and tracking handed over (§7).\n- [ ] Deliverables formally accepted against their acceptance criteria.\n- [ ] Requirements satisfied and traced; solution evaluated against the need (§5).\n- [ ] Scope, schedule, and cost reconciled to baselines; variances explained and closed (§6).\n- [ ] Stakeholders engaged and expectations aligned through close.\n- [ ] Risks and issues closed or transferred with named owners.\n- [ ] Quality verified for both deliverables and process.\n- [ ] Sustainability and ethics addressed; AI controls satisfied where AI was used or delivered (§11).\n- [ ] Contracts settled; resources released; team feedback given.\n- [ ] Lessons captured and shared.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Glossary\n\n- **AC / EV / PV** — actual cost / earned value / planned value (§6).\n- **BAC / EAC / ETC / VAC** — budget at completion / estimate at completion / estimate to complete / variance at completion.\n- **CPI / SPI / TCPI** — cost / schedule / to-complete performance index.\n- **ES / SV(t) / SPI(t)** — earned schedule and its time-based variance and index (§6.5).\n- **Control account** — an accountable chunk of work with one owner, used for measurement.\n- **WBS / OBS** — work breakdown structure / organizational breakdown structure.\n- **EVM** — earned value management: integrated scope/schedule/cost performance measurement.\n- **Benefit / dis-benefit** — a measurable improvement / worsening an outcome produces for a stakeholder.\n- **Benefits register / benefits map** — the list of planned benefits / a model linking outputs to objectives.\n- **Functional / non-functional requirement** — what the solution does / how well it does it.\n- **Contingency vs. management reserve** — budget for identified risks vs. for the unforeseen.\n- **Predictive / iterative / hybrid** — plan-first / increment-and-learn / a deliberate mix.\n- **Human in the loop** — required human review and intervention in AI-driven decisions.\n- **Program / portfolio** — related work managed for collective benefit / a managed set of work chosen for strategic value.\n\n---\n\n## 15. References and further reading\n\nThis guide synthesizes general practice. For authoritative detail, consult primary sources. URLs are given where verified.\n\n- ISO 21502:2020 — *Project, programme and portfolio management — Guidance on project management.*\n- ISO 21500:2021 — *Project, programme and portfolio management — Context and concepts.*\n- ISO 31000:2018 — *Risk management — Guidelines.*\n- ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018 — *Systems and software engineering — Life cycle processes — Requirements engineering.* https://www.iso.org/standard/72089.html\n- ANSI/EIA-748 — *Earned Value Management Systems* (the EVM systems standard); see also public government EVM guides.\n- Earned Schedule — a public, openly described extension of EVM into the time domain. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earned_schedule\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0, NIST AI 100-1, 2023) — functions: govern, map, measure, manage. https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — *Information technology — Artificial intelligence — Management system.* https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\n- OECD AI Principles — intergovernmental principles for trustworthy AI. https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\n- EU AI Act — risk-tiered EU regulation of AI systems (consult the official EU text for current obligations).\n- Managing Successful Programmes (MSP) and Cranfield benefits-management research — for benefits realization.\n- *A Guide to the Business Analysis Body of Knowledge (BABOK® Guide)*, IIBA — for business analysis.\n- *A Guide to the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK® Guide)*, PMI; PRINCE2® (AXELOS); and *The Scrum Guide* — additional widely used project frameworks.\n\nTrademarks belong to their respective owners and are named here only for reference.\n\n---\n\n## License\n\nCopyright 2026 Sebastian Maute, Acme GmbH\nContact: sebastian.maute@example.com\n\nLicensed under the European Union Public Licence v1.2 (the \"Licence\");\nyou may not use this file except in compliance with the Licence.\nYou may obtain a copy of the Licence at\n\n    https://interoperable-europe.ec.europa.eu/collection/eupl/eupl-text-eupl-12\n\nUnless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software\ndistributed under the Licence is distributed on an \"AS IS\" BASIS,\nWITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.\nSee the Licence for the specific language governing permissions and\nlimitations under the Licence.\n\nThe full EUPL-1.2 licence text is maintained at the distribution level — in the LICENSE file of the application this document is bundled with — and is not duplicated here. When redistributing or adapting, comply with the Licence, retain this header, and state any changes you make. This is a documentation work: the licence applies to this document and its derivatives. Provided \"as is,\" without warranty; not professional or legal advice.\n";
